win32u: name the rectangle a frame paint excludes.

One line per non-client paint of a visible window, giving the client rectangle
nc_paint keeps its frame off. On a channel of its own -- WINEDEBUG=+nonclient --
because the file default `win` traces every window message, which would bury a
once-per-frame line and slow Wine enough to change the painting being observed.

This is an instrument, not a fix, and it is what disproved the theory it was
written for. A blank caption-height band sits across the top of every Chromium
window under Vessel. The theory was that nc_paint excludes the client in the
wrong coordinate space: it asks for COORDS_SCREEN and hands the region to a
DCX_WINDOW device context, which looks like a space mismatch. Changing it to
COORDS_WINDOW left the band exactly as it was, and reading the DC code shows
the original was right -- the region is combined into the visible region while
that is still in screen coordinates (dce.c, get_dc_ex) and only afterwards
mapped to DC space (clipping.c, set_visible_region):

    NtGdiOffsetRgn( hrgn, -vis_rect->left, -vis_rect->top );

so COORDS_WINDOW was a regression that stopped the client being excluded at
all. Measured on device, VS Code window 0x1009e: the excluded rectangle reads
(371,140)-(1549,929) in screen space, which is correct, while the band is 34
screen pixels of pure white -- about 29 guest pixels, one SM_CYCAPTION -- and
sits inside the client area. It is not painted by this function.

Kept because the next theory needs the same number, and because a trace that
has already killed one explanation is worth more than the explanation was.

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
index 9404a87664d..207e0d9679c 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -30,6 +30,12 @@
 
 WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(win);
 
+/* Vessel: a channel of its own for the non-client paint trace below, so it can
+ * be read without +win. The default channel here traces every window message,
+ * which would bury a once-per-frame line and slow Wine enough to change the
+ * painting being observed. */
+WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(nonclient);
+
 #define WINE_MOUSE_HANDLE       ((HANDLE)1)
 #define WINE_KEYBOARD_HANDLE    ((HANDLE)2)
 
@@ -1763,6 +1769,27 @@ static void nc_paint( HWND hwnd, HRGN clip )
     get_client_rect_rel( hwnd, COORDS_SCREEN, &rectClient, get_thread_dpi() );
     hrgn = NtGdiCreateRectRgn( rectClient.left, rectClient.top,
                                rectClient.right, rectClient.bottom );
+    /* Vessel: name the rectangle the frame paint refuses to draw over.
+     *
+     * SCREEN is the right space and this trace is what proved it. The region
+     * is combined into the DC visible region while that is still in screen
+     * coordinates -- dce.c get_dc_ex -- and only then offset to DC space by
+     * clipping.c set_visible_region:
+     *
+     *     NtGdiOffsetRgn( hrgn, -vis_rect->left, -vis_rect->top );
+     *
+     * so a window-relative rectangle here lands at the wrong place on screen
+     * and stops excluding the client at all. That was tried, on the theory
+     * that it explained a blank caption-height band across the top of every
+     * Chromium window; the band was unchanged and the change was a regression.
+     * The band is inside the client and is not drawn by this function.
+     *
+     * Kept as an instrument rather than removed with the theory: it is one
+     * line per non-client paint of a visible window and it names, in the units
+     * the bug is argued in, exactly which pixels the frame agrees not to
+     * touch. WINEDEBUG=+nonclient prints it. */
+    TRACE_(nonclient)( "hwnd %p exclude client %s (screen)\n", hwnd,
+                       wine_dbgstr_rect( &rectClient ) );
 
     if (clip > (HRGN)1)
     {
